WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.590
The economics of state-administered death.

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:06.465
MAID costs eight thousand, one hundred and fifty dollars per death.

00:00:06.465 --> 00:00:11.568
Palliative care costs twenty-eight thousand, eight hundred and thirty-five dollars per patient.

00:00:11.568 --> 00:00:18.090
The government saves twenty thousand, six hundred and eighty-five dollars every time someone dies instead of receiving care.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.681
All figures from the Parliamentary Budget Officer and Health Canada.

00:00:21.681 --> 00:00:30.534
At scale, seventy-six thousand, four hundred and seventy-five cumulative MAID deaths represent one point five eight billion dollars in avoided palliative care.

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:35.659
The Parliamentary Budget Officer estimated one hundred and forty-nine million in annual savings.

00:00:35.659 --> 00:00:41.249
The projected savings trajectory reaches one point two seven trillion dollars by twenty forty-seven.

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:45.920
One hundred and two top MAID doctors have been paid eighteen million dollars collectively.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.465
Physician compensation.

00:00:47.465 --> 00:00:50.352
MAID is billed through provincial health insurance plans.

00:00:50.352 --> 00:00:55.420
Total physician compensation ranges from fifteen hundred to thirty-five hundred dollars per case.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.534
Initial assessment: four hundred to eight hundred.

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:01.579
Second assessment: four hundred to eight hundred.

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:05.818
The lethal injection procedure itself: five hundred to fifteen hundred.

00:01:05.818 --> 00:01:09.568
Documentation and reporting: two hundred to four hundred.

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:11.159
Provider concentration.

00:01:11.159 --> 00:01:16.431
One hundred and two physicians collectively earned approximately eighteen million dollars from MAID billing.

00:01:16.431 --> 00:01:23.806
That averages three hundred and seventy-three deaths per physician — roughly one MAID procedure every business day for one and a half years.

00:01:23.806 --> 00:01:29.147
Ontario regulatory bodies tracked four hundred and twenty-eight legal and procedural violations.

00:01:29.147 --> 00:01:32.288
Zero were referred to police for criminal investigation.

00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:34.147
The structural incentive problem.

00:01:34.147 --> 00:01:39.943
Prime Minister Mark Carney holds six point eight million dollars in Brookfield Asset Management options.

00:01:39.943 --> 00:01:45.340
Brookfield is one of the world's largest owners of seniors housing and long-term care infrastructure.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.534
MAID directly reduces demand for long-term care beds.

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:54.113
The government saves twenty thousand, six hundred and eighty-five dollars per death versus palliative care.

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:59.363
Provincial billing creates volume incentives with no mechanism to flag high-volume providers.
